  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) are both recommended as equal first-line options to treat high blood pressure, but generally have a safer and better-tolerated side effect profile.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "generally have a safer and better-tolerated side effect profile"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE inhibitors (names ending in \"-pril,\" like lisinopril) stop the body from making high levels of angiotensin II, a hormone that narrows blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "(names ending in \"-sartan,\" like losartan) block the receptor sites where angiotensin II attaches, which relaxes blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Both lower blood pressure by relaxing blood vessels and reducing strain on the heart."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness and Safety"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Effectiveness: Large studies show that both drug classes lower blood pressure and protect the heart and kidneys equally well."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects: ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent dry cough and carry a small risk of (severe swelling of the tissue under the skin) because they affect a chemical called bradykinin. ARBs do not affect bradykinin, meaning they do not cause the dry cough and have a much lower risk of angioedema.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2021/08/03/19/47/comparative-effectiveness-of-ace-inhibitors-and-arbs"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cost: ACE inhibitors are often generic and less expensive, whereas ARBs can cost slightly more."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you currently experiencing any side effects from a blood pressure medication?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have any other conditions like kidney disease or diabetes?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you discuss these options with your doctor."
      }
    ],
